Yes. I would make this a dedicated LeaseOS Commercial Office Administration Hub rather than scattering client, vendor, disposal, accounting, tax and document records across unrelated modules.

The goal should be simple: every dollar, load, document, signature, vendor, client, disposal movement and adjustment should be traceable back to its source record and auditable without the office having to reconstruct the job afterward.

1. Master Client Registry

Every customer should have one canonical account record, with multiple divisions, contacts, sites and billing arrangements underneath it.

Client data	What LeaseOS should track

Client ID	Permanent internal ID such as CLI-000184
Legal name	Contract/invoice entity
Operating/trade name	Field-facing company name
Business number	Tax/accounting reference where applicable
Tax status	GST/HST/PST rules, exemptions, certificates
Billing addresses	Separate AP/remittance/project addresses
Contacts	Dispatch, field, AP, safety, management
Sites/projects	Rigs, leases, plants, construction sites, yards
Client PO requirements	Required before dispatch/invoice
Cost codes	Client-specific codes
Rate agreements	Effective dates and version history
Payment terms	Net 15/30/45 etc.
Credit limit	Optional
Insurance requirements	COI/endorsement requirements
Contract/MSA	Linked current and historical versions
Special billing rules	Minimums, standby, travel, OT, fuel surcharge
Job-number rules	Client numbering + LeaseOS numbering
Invoice delivery	Email/API/portal/export
Client portal access	Authorized client users
Status	prospect/active/on-hold/suspended/archived
Risk flags	overdue, contract expired, insurance issue, dispute


A client should never be deleted once referenced financially. It becomes archived/inactive so historical invoices and job records remain valid.


